{"reference":"Isaiah 26","book":"Isaiah","chapter":26,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A Question About Verse 19","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Isaiah 26:19's promise that the dead will live and arise is often read alongside Isaiah 25-27 and 65-66, where death’s defeat, bodily revival, renewed Jerusalem, transformed longevity, and new-heavens-and-earth imagery also appear. The extent and meaning of this connection remain disputed, but these passages became foundational for later Jewish and Christian hope concerning resurrection and new creation.","verse_refs":["Isaiah 26:19"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_026_e17b98b215a48c1cd53604ea"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Isaiah’s Textual Witnesses","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The Great Isaiah Scroll is an almost complete witness to all sixty-six chapters of Isaiah. It is broadly aligned with the Masoretic tradition, though it contains thousands of spelling and substantive differences; other Isaiah manuscripts and interpretive writings from Qumran also show that Isaiah was actively copied and interpreted.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_026_d99be88ce927c4bb990b420c"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}